WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.893
Four journeys. No traveller here is a real
person: each is a composite,

00:00:05.961 --> 00:00:07.960
built on published facts.

00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:10.976
Riyadh, late at night.

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:15.828
A family drives its car onto the train and
finds its sleeper cabin.

00:00:15.896 --> 00:00:18.807
They wake in the far north, at Qurayyat.

00:00:19.157 --> 00:00:23.869
And this part is real: on the North Line,
night trains have sleeper cabins,

00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:27.637
and carry passengers' cars between Riyadh
and Qurayyat.

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:33.843
Thursday afternoon. A consultant in Riyadh
heads for the airport, as most weeks,

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.273
for the Jeddah flight.

00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:39.274
In 2025, Jeddah–Riyadh came fifth in OAG's

00:00:39.341 --> 00:00:42.579
top ten air routes by scheduled seats:

00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:48.053
about 9.8 million seats both ways. Seats
are the seats on offer,

00:00:48.121 --> 00:00:50.703
not the number of passengers.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.220
Jeddah. A family packs for a summer week
in Abha, up on the escarpment.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:03.061
By car, passes like Aqabat Shaar: 11
tunnels and 32 bridges. By air,

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:09.315
one airline's schedule set Abha–Jeddah at
an hour and 15 minutes. By train,

00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:11.170
there is no way at all.

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.013
And in January 2025,

00:01:14.081 --> 00:01:20.335
the chief executive of Asir's development
authority said the official target is 9.1

00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:22.807
million visitors a year by 2030.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.860
Back to Riyadh. A student finishes her
last exam and books a seat home to Tabuk.

00:01:28.927 --> 00:01:33.800
Ahead of her: the plane, or the long road
north-west. The train doesn't go there.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.007
Four journeys, one question:

00:01:37.075 --> 00:01:40.757
how does rail become a real choice on more
of these journeys?

00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:44.800
An independent study tried to answer, in
ranges, not promises.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.927
The Hejaz Railway reached Madinah in 1908;

00:01:49.995 --> 00:01:55.160
the part inside today's Kingdom stayed
closed after 1920.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.069
And in October 1951,

00:01:58.136 --> 00:02:03.767
King Abdulaziz inaugurated the railway
line between Riyadh and Dammam.

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:11.549
Today's passenger map reads quickly. The
East Line to Dammam. And the North Line,

00:02:11.616 --> 00:02:15.911
about 1,250 kilometres, via Qassim, Hail
and Al-Jouf,

00:02:15.979 --> 00:02:20.247
with passenger trains reaching Qurayyat
since 2022.

00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:26.140
And the Haramain train links Makkah,
Jeddah and Madinah.

00:02:26.208 --> 00:02:28.237
It opened in October 2018,

00:02:28.305 --> 00:02:33.833
and from early 2020 its trains have run at
up to 300 kilometres an hour.

00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:40.026
And Riyadh now has a metro: six lines and
85 stations,

00:02:40.094 --> 00:02:44.513
opened between December 2024 and January
2025.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.719
So who is off this map? The study

00:02:48.787 --> 00:02:53.494
screened all 150 governorates. In the 2022
census,

00:02:53.562 --> 00:03:00.045
Taif governorate had about 913 thousand
people; Tabuk, 624 thousand;

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:03.710
Khamis Mushait, 601 thousand; Abha and

00:03:03.777 --> 00:03:07.263
Najran, 422 thousand each; and Jazan,

00:03:07.331 --> 00:03:13.660
201 thousand. Not one has a passenger
station within 10 kilometres.

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:20.732
The committed lines, the Landbridge and
the Saudi section of the GCC Railway,

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:26.267
are announced or under procurement. None
of them carries passengers yet.

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:28.923
Even with them,

00:03:28.991 --> 00:03:35.643
about 26.10 million of 35.93 million
people live within two hours of a station:

00:03:35.711 --> 00:03:39.987
a figure for display only, not part of the
scoring.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.921
And the country's population is growing.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:50.840
The 2022 census counted 32,175,224 people.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.722
By mid-2024, the General Authority

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:58.870
for Statistics estimated 35.3 million.

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:03.846
After The Line designs were launched in
July 2022,

00:04:03.914 --> 00:04:09.925
His Royal Highness Crown Prince Mohammed
bin Salman told journalists the aim is for

00:04:09.992 --> 00:04:14.180
the Kingdom's population to reach 50 to 60
million by 2030,

00:04:14.247 --> 00:04:17.479
about half of them Saudis. These are
remarks,

00:04:17.547 --> 00:04:21.107
not a target published in an official
programme.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.802
On Riyadh, the president of the Royal
Commission

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.843
for Riyadh City told Reuters in July 2020
the

00:04:28.911 --> 00:04:31.309
idea was a city of 15 million by 2030.

00:04:31.377 --> 00:04:34.500
Later reports say the figure is under
review.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:39.516
As for the Vision 2030 document, one of
its goals, in paraphrase,

00:04:39.584 --> 00:04:41.835
is to raise the capacity to receive Umrah

00:04:41.903 --> 00:04:44.700
visitors from 8 million to 30 million
every year.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.462
Another of its goals:

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.970
three Saudi cities among the top-ranked
100 cities in the world.

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:53.643
It speaks of completing,

00:04:53.711 --> 00:04:57.483
improving and linking infrastructure
internally and across borders,

00:04:57.551 --> 00:05:01.259
and of building shared road and railway
networks with the Gulf states.

00:05:01.327 --> 00:05:04.207
This is the meaning of the text, not its
exact wording.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.044
A country growing like this raises a fair
question:

00:05:09.112 --> 00:05:13.440
must airports and highways be the only
ways to travel across it?

00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:18.204
Who would ride? Business trips between
Riyadh and Jeddah;

00:05:18.272 --> 00:05:22.833
families heading for the cool of Asir; and
students and families going north.

00:05:24.233 --> 00:05:29.973
Start with the busiest pair. In 2050, with
the Landbridge running,

00:05:30.041 --> 00:05:36.715
rail takes about 0.42 million of 4.66
million one-way trips a year between the

00:05:36.782 --> 00:05:41.553
Riyadh and Jeddah areas. Air keeps about
3.47 million.

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:46.232
Why so few? In the study's corridor
sketches,

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.530
air stays ahead door to door in every rail
case calculated,

00:05:50.598 --> 00:05:54.038
even a non-stop train at 350 kilometres an
hour.

00:05:54.106 --> 00:05:59.807
Rail's strength here is in the towns in
between, and in city-centre stations.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.957
The film's thread is connecting the
country,

00:06:04.024 --> 00:06:08.713
not only the two holy cities or the
largest cities. The study tests it simply:

00:06:08.781 --> 00:06:13.240
who can reach one of the five big metros
within 120 minutes, door to door?

00:06:14.633 --> 00:06:19.857
None of the layouts tested brings any new
area within 60 minutes of a big metro.

00:06:19.924 --> 00:06:23.913
The distances here are a nation's
distances, not suburbs.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.893
So does a railway create trips, or only
move them?

00:06:28.961 --> 00:06:33.715
The committed lines move trips between
modes and destinations;

00:06:33.783 --> 00:06:37.980
the Kingdom-wide total is the same with or
without them.

00:06:39.367 --> 00:06:43.262
And if cheaper or faster travel generated
extra trips,

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.053
the model adds 0.2 to 1.1 million trips a
year in 2050,

00:06:47.121 --> 00:06:50.567
which changes the overall result only a
little.

00:06:51.967 --> 00:06:57.111
Today, air carries a large share of the
country's travel. In 2025,

00:06:57.179 --> 00:07:03.650
the General Authority of Civil Aviation
counted 65 million domestic travellers,

00:07:03.718 --> 00:07:06.087
of about 140.9 million in all.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.134
According to OAG,

00:07:09.202 --> 00:07:15.715
Jeddah–Riyadh was the fastest-growing of
the top ten routes in 2025:

00:07:15.782 --> 00:07:19.980
up 13 percent on 2024, and 22 percent on
2019.

00:07:21.367 --> 00:07:26.778
And there is a lesson from Japan: despite
its extensive high-speed rail network,

00:07:26.846 --> 00:07:29.517
the world's second and third busiest air

00:07:29.585 --> 00:07:32.727
routes in 2025 were Japanese domestic
routes.

00:07:34.133 --> 00:07:35.222
In December 2025,

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:40.468
Saudi Arabia and Qatar signed an agreement
for a 785-kilometre high-speed line,

00:07:40.536 --> 00:07:45.560
with stations including Hofuf and Dammam,
and a target trip of about two hours.

00:07:45.627 --> 00:07:47.093
Announced, not built.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:54.156
The Landbridge, about 900 kilometres of
new railway between Riyadh and Jeddah,

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:58.580
is reported as due by 2034. It too is
announced, not existing.

00:07:59.967 --> 00:08:04.857
How do you test a railway that doesn't
exist? You start with the ground.

00:08:04.924 --> 00:08:10.066
Every hypothetical line drawn for the
study was routed over real terrain,

00:08:10.134 --> 00:08:15.847
on 30-metre elevation data, at the least
cost that slopes, dunes and wadis allow.

00:08:17.234 --> 00:08:23.746
When the method was tried on three lines
in service, lengths came within 5 percent,

00:08:23.814 --> 00:08:27.561
but paths were 3 to 10 kilometres off on
average.

00:08:27.629 --> 00:08:31.634
So every route here is a rough sketch, not
a design.

00:08:33.034 --> 00:08:39.462
Tunnels, bridges and earthworks were
measured. Take the descent from Taif:

00:08:39.529 --> 00:08:42.100
in two of the sketches via Taif,

00:08:42.168 --> 00:08:47.682
61.9 straight-line kilometres become 91.4
kilometres of track,

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:52.114
with 16.2 kilometres of tunnel and 14.8 of
bridges.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:56.473
Near Makkah, one rule shaped the sketches:

00:08:56.541 --> 00:09:01.879
a train running between Riyadh and Jeddah
stays outside the Haram boundary,

00:09:01.947 --> 00:09:05.765
so every ticketed passenger can complete
the journey.

00:09:05.832 --> 00:09:10.140
The Haramain train already serves Makkah,
from Ar Rusayfah.

00:09:11.534 --> 00:09:17.823
Costs come from the study's own register,
for double-track electrified railway,

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:23.694
not from press-release figures. Each cost
is a range: low, middle and high.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.152
Big rail projects often go over budget,

00:09:28.219 --> 00:09:33.445
so the study adds an uplift from
international reference classes,

00:09:33.513 --> 00:09:35.808
one of them 355 rail projects,

00:09:35.876 --> 00:09:40.980
and shows contingency separately instead
of quietly adding it.

00:09:42.367 --> 00:09:46.329
Then the search. Starting from the
committed lines,

00:09:46.397 --> 00:09:52.649
the study searched combinations of 71
candidate corridors: new stations, spurs,

00:09:52.716 --> 00:09:55.647
commuter-rail belts and trunk lines.

00:09:57.034 --> 00:10:01.254
Each candidate network was scored by one
frozen appraisal model,

00:10:01.321 --> 00:10:04.527
which we call the judge, over 10,000
random draws,

00:10:04.595 --> 00:10:09.438
its fingerprint checked before and after
every run. Its score is cautious:

00:10:09.506 --> 00:10:12.234
the worst of three population scenarios.

00:10:13.634 --> 00:10:18.696
Classical optimisers searched, and so did
an automated research loop:

00:10:18.764 --> 00:10:21.774
one change at a time, a written
hypothesis,

00:10:21.842 --> 00:10:24.834
and a change kept only if the judge
agreed.

00:10:26.234 --> 00:10:32.634
Every attempt left a row in a register:
47,108 rows in the final search.

00:10:34.034 --> 00:10:39.017
At every review checkpoint, a person read
the results and decided the next step.

00:10:39.085 --> 00:10:41.874
The machine searched, but it did not
decide.

00:10:43.267 --> 00:10:47.914
So does the railway pay back its cost? For
every riyal spent,

00:10:47.981 --> 00:10:52.062
how many riyals come back? If the answer
is below one,

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:56.588
the line does not repay its cost. For the
committed lines,

00:10:56.656 --> 00:11:00.427
the middle answer is about 0.3 riyals per
riyal.

00:11:01.834 --> 00:11:08.150
The search put forward three options, and
none is a recommendation to build.

00:11:08.218 --> 00:11:13.536
The central case: new stations at Al
Jubayl, Dhahran and Duruma,

00:11:13.604 --> 00:11:18.025
costing 0.8 to 5.3 billion riyals. In the
middle case,

00:11:18.092 --> 00:11:23.610
each riyal spent brings back about 6
riyals, and even if costs rise,

00:11:23.678 --> 00:11:25.274
it still pays back.

00:11:26.667 --> 00:11:30.092
The upside case adds the spur to

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:35.332
Al-Kharj, costing 6.2 to 41.3 billion
riyals;

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:42.049
in the middle case, each riyal brings back
about 2.3 riyals.

00:11:42.117 --> 00:11:45.677
But if costs come in high, it does

00:11:45.744 --> 00:11:51.387
not pay back in two of three population
scenarios.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:59.007
The reach alternative adds a commuter-rail
belt around Riyadh:

00:11:59.075 --> 00:12:03.423
4.25 million people within 120 minutes,
door

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.444
to door, of the five big metros,

00:12:06.511 --> 00:12:10.395
against 3.56 million. Each riyal brings

00:12:10.462 --> 00:12:14.113
back about 1.1 riyals, just above one;

00:12:14.181 --> 00:12:18.480
at high cost, it does not pay back in any
case.

00:12:19.867 --> 00:12:23.196
Who pays? No option covers its cost from
tickets:

00:12:23.263 --> 00:12:27.006
their revenue covers only part of running
and renewal,

00:12:27.074 --> 00:12:31.547
so whatever the delivery method, the state
pays the difference.

00:12:32.934 --> 00:12:38.231
And what comes back? For the committed
lines over 60 years,

00:12:38.299 --> 00:12:41.954
the model estimates people's savings in

00:12:42.022 --> 00:12:45.567
travel time and cost, in today's money,

00:12:45.635 --> 00:12:50.494
at 2.3 to 19.3 billion riyals. And the
safety benefit,

00:12:50.562 --> 00:12:56.735
deaths and injuries avoided, valued at
standard appraisal values,

00:12:56.803 --> 00:12:58.774
at 0.6 to 7.4 billion.

00:13:00.167 --> 00:13:05.746
And there is a much bigger figure: a wider
effect on the economy,

00:13:05.814 --> 00:13:09.368
roughly 114.6 to 267.7 billion riyals in

00:13:09.436 --> 00:13:12.778
the middle case for the three options.

00:13:12.845 --> 00:13:17.359
But the method is not reliable on these
large areas,

00:13:17.427 --> 00:13:22.647
so the study keeps it out of the options'
ranking entirely.

00:13:24.034 --> 00:13:28.760
And a direct railway between Riyadh and
Jeddah? A side study,

00:13:28.827 --> 00:13:34.963
not the official ranking, drew five routes
in place of the Landbridge. In each,

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:39.098
a riyal brings back only about 0.2 riyals,
below one,

00:13:39.166 --> 00:13:42.481
so on these numbers none pays back its
cost.

00:13:42.549 --> 00:13:49.034
That helps explain why there is no direct
railway between Riyadh and Jeddah today.

00:13:50.434 --> 00:13:53.641
They differ in whom they reach.

00:13:53.709 --> 00:14:00.066
The direct sketch adds no one to the
national two-hour reach;

00:14:00.133 --> 00:14:04.475
the three via Taif add about 0.23 million;

00:14:04.542 --> 00:14:07.246
and the sketch through the

00:14:07.314 --> 00:14:13.234
Landbridge's announced stations, about
0.31 million.

00:14:14.634 --> 00:14:20.972
Within 120 minutes of the five big metros,
only two add anyone:

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:26.885
about 93,693 people with a gateway
south-west of Makkah,

00:14:26.953 --> 00:14:31.634
and about 109,007 with a station at
Al-Jumum.

00:14:33.034 --> 00:14:37.805
Now the far north and the far south:
Tabuk, Abha, Khamis Mushait,

00:14:37.872 --> 00:14:44.234
Najran and Jazan. On today's map, none has
a passenger station within 10 kilometres.

00:14:45.634 --> 00:14:48.601
And as a comparison, not a recommendation,

00:14:48.668 --> 00:14:51.719
the study also built one connected
network:

00:14:51.787 --> 00:14:55.074
the cheapest 16 additions that join 18
cities.

00:14:56.467 --> 00:15:00.491
In it, a line of about 460 kilometres
links Tabuk to Sakaka,

00:15:00.559 --> 00:15:03.664
with a short spur joining it to the North
Line.

00:15:03.732 --> 00:15:07.004
Abha and Khamis Mushait connect east to
Najran,

00:15:07.072 --> 00:15:10.345
north through Bisha to Taif and the
Landbridge,

00:15:10.412 --> 00:15:12.667
and down the mountains to Jazan.

00:15:14.067 --> 00:15:19.305
That puts 32.7 million people within two
hours of a station,

00:15:19.372 --> 00:15:24.147
against 26.1 million with the committed
lines alone.

00:15:25.534 --> 00:15:28.989
And how long is the trip by train, door to
door,

00:15:29.057 --> 00:15:35.454
from these cities to the nearest big city?
The study did not calculate these trips.

00:15:36.867 --> 00:15:41.565
The price of this connected network: in
the middle case,

00:15:41.633 --> 00:15:48.427
each riyal spent brings back only about
0.06 riyals, six halalas, far below one.

00:15:49.834 --> 00:15:53.652
A field survey and observed demand could
change all of this,

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:57.538
and there are review gates before each
construction start,

00:15:57.605 --> 00:16:00.714
showing where the decision could be
revisited.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:07.054
These are four journeys. A night train
north that runs today.

00:16:07.122 --> 00:16:12.962
An air shuttle that rail does not beat. A
climb to Abha and a road to Tabuk,

00:16:13.029 --> 00:16:14.900
still off the rail map.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:21.024
A railway that connects more of the
country is possible. Is it worth building?

00:16:21.091 --> 00:16:25.962
That is for those who decide; the evidence
is here for anyone who wants to read it,

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:28.020
every figure sourced on screen.
